    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I am currently overseeing the acquisition of a new collection of rare 19th-century manuscripts for our university library here in Belgium. I am trying to decide whether we should implement the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) or the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) for this specific collection. Could you provide a comparative analysis of the two systems, specifically regarding their granularity for historical manuscripts and their scalability for growing archival collections?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) is generally preferred for rare historical manuscripts due to its superior granularity for specific subjects like 19th-century Europe, as seen in the K subclass under Library of Congress, the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) offers better scalability for expanding archival collections through its manual extension methods. The LCC provides detailed cross-referencing for historical documents, whereas DDC's reliance on a single Dewey Placement Number can become cumbersome for complex archival materials. Consequently, most Belgian university libraries adopting the LCC also utilize Local Classified Sections (LCS) to mimic the agility of Dewey's scale when managing heterogeneous collections, ensuring precise yet flexible organization.",
      "source": "target",
